# renki-manifest. Design

What reads a manifest off the disk. Every rule it applies, which file is a
layer, the order, the `meta` table, the merge, the predicate, the glob, is
`renki-config`'s and asked of it; this crate does the i/o those rules need and
nothing else. It is `#![no_std]` and allocates nothing: the files are read
through the C library, the text goes into memory the caller gives, and TOML is
read by `tomlfuse-parse`. A tool that only reads its manifest takes this crate
and the contracts, and nothing of the launcher.

Its reading implements the contracts' store, so it will speak notko and the
stack's string and number types as `renki-config` does. Its reading, its store
and its deserialiser wait, with the contracts' port, on those crates being on
the registry; the host, the one unsafe module and the POSIX reader do not.
Until the stack is on the registry the crate is outside the reach of the
stack's bare-type lints, which read no crate of it, and nothing in it is
called passing them: the host speaks `core`'s `Result` and `Option` and the C
library's integers, and is ported with the rest.

## Reading a manifest

One call reads a manifest with everything below: its layers, their includes,
the predicates that decide which are read, and the merge. A tool writes its
type, gives the room the text is read into, and wires nothing else; a manifest
it reads gets layering by default.

```rust
#[derive(serde::Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
struct App<'a> {
    name: &'a str,
    hooks: Hooks<'a>,
}

let mut rooms = Rooms::<{ 64 * 1024 }, 4096>::new();
let app: App<'_> = renki_manifest::read("app.toml", &mut rooms.room())?;
```

`renki_manifest::read::<T>(path, room)` is `Manifest::find(path, room)`
followed by `Manifest::read::<T>`. The parts are public for a caller that
wants the dump, the provenance or the stack, and the one call is what every
other use takes.

## The room

`Room<'r>` is the memory a read uses, borrowed from the caller as four
slices: the text of every layer read, back to back; a decode shelf as long as
the text; the nodes of `tomlfuse-parse`'s index, which is each document as it
was read; and a path shelf, where the base's path and each joined include path
are written with their terminating NUL for the host. `Rooms<TEXT, NODES>` owns
the four in fixed arrays, on the stack or in a static, for a caller with no
allocator, and lends a `Room`. Every string a read hands out borrows from the
room.

The size of each file is known before its bytes are read, so a file that does
not fit is refused with `BadRead::Room`, naming the file and the text and
nodes the room needs; a caller with an allocator gives slices of that size and
reads again. `tomlfuse_parse::nodes_for` answers how many nodes a text of a
given length can need at most, so a room sized by it never runs out of nodes.
A decoded TOML string is never longer than its source, since every escape
decodes to at most its own length and a line-ending backslash removes bytes,
so a decode shelf as long as the text never runs out either.

## The host

`Host` is the i/o a read needs, one trait over the operating system: read a
whole file into the room, list a directory's entries, resolve a path to its
canonical form into a buffer, copy one environment variable's value into
memory the caller gives, and, with the feature `processes`, run
`sh -c <command>` in a directory and answer its exit. Every path it takes is
a NUL-terminated one from the room's path shelf, and a `BadRead` names the
file by its place there. `Unix` implements it over the C library through the
`libc` crate with its default features off, for linux, macOS, freebsd,
netbsd, openbsd and illumos; a target with no implementation does not build.
Every `unsafe` block of the crate is in this module.

A file is opened without blocking and refused with `BadRead::NotAFile` unless
it is a regular file, so a directory or a FIFO named like a layer is refused
and never read or waited on. A directory listing the C library ends with an
error is refused, so no layer goes missing in silence. A variable's value is
copied, never borrowed from the process's environment, which a thread of the
program may change while a manifest is read.

A shell runs in its directory through a wrapping
`sh -c 'cd -- "$1" || exit 125; exec sh -c "$2" >/dev/null'`, so no target
needs a spawn call some C libraries lack. Its standard output goes to
`/dev/null` and its standard error is the reader's, in both readers, so what a
predicate prints never reaches a dump. A directory the shell cannot enter
exits 125, which a predicate neither holds nor fails on, so it is refused.

## Finding the layers

`Manifest::find` takes the base's path. It reads the base and, where there is
one, `name.defaults.ext`, then lists the base's directory and keeps every file
`renki_config::layer_of` calls a layer. From each layer it reads, and from the
base and `defaults`, it reads `meta` first and follows `meta.overlays.include`:
each entry relative to the file that names it, a directory where it ends in
`/` (its files listed, the layers among them kept), else a glob matched with
`renki_config::glob_matches` against the entries of its directory. A name a
glob matches that is no layer of this manifest, a directory among them, is
refused by name; in a directory an entry lists, what is no layer is passed
over. A layer that is no regular file is refused by name. A directory entry
naming no directory is refused, naming the entry and the file; a glob that
matches nothing, in a directory that is there or not, reads nothing. An entry
is joined to its file's directory and resolved by the file system, a link and
a `..` after it included, never as text, and a refusal names it as joined,
which is what the manifest wrote. A path holding a newline is refused where a
reader would read or name it: the base, an include entry, a name a glob
matches and a layer's name, since neither reader can name it on a line; a
name holding one that is no layer and that nothing names is passed over like
any other.
The `defaults` layer is read from beside the base only: its predicates decide
which layers are read, so it is found before any include is followed, and one
an include reaches is refused by name.

Following goes to a fixed point. A layer whose `meta.when` does not hold is
not read, and its includes are not followed. A file reached twice, by any
path, is read once, by its canonical path, so an include cycle ends by itself.
Two files carrying one layer word are refused, naming both. The layers then
take their places by `renki_config::Order`, lowest first: `defaults`, the
base, the rest by `Order::rank`. Every layer found takes a place, read or not,
so a layer the order has no place for is refused whether or not its
`meta.when` holds.

A layer in `meta.overlays.untracked` is checked against git where the
manifest's directory is in a work tree, once per file, for every layer found,
read or not, since a tracked secret is tracked whatever decides its reading:
one that git tracks is refused, naming the file and saying
`git rm --cached <file>`. The check is
`git ls-files --error-unmatch -- <file>` through the host's shell. Nothing is
written to `.gitignore`. Git is no requirement of reading a manifest: where the
directory is in no work tree, where no `git` is on the path, or where `git`
fails to answer, the check is skipped in silence and the read goes on. The
check spawns a process, so it is run only with the feature `processes`;
without it the check is not run, and the dump opens with the line
`# the untracked check was not run`.

## Answering the predicates

`Ambient` is this crate's `renki_config::Answers`, over the host: the variable
for `Env`, and for `Shell` the exit of `sh -c` in the manifest's directory.
The shell half is behind the feature `processes`, off by default, the one
gate on spawning a process; a manifest that names a shell predicate on a
build without it is refused by name. `renki`, the launcher, turns it on. `holds` runs every
time a use site is read; this crate keeps no answer.

## Merging and the schema

The merged view is not built. `Manifest::view` is the checked
`renki_config::Stack` of read layers,
lowest first, each a `renki_config::Layer` over its text in the room, and every
key of it is answered by `renki_config::stacked`, which applies `meet`,
`meta.replace` and `meta.unset` as the rule says and names the layer that
answered. The view carries no `meta`.

`Manifest::read::<T>` deserialises the view into the tool's own type through
`serde` with its default features off: a deserializer walks the stack's keys
and hands each value from the layer `stacked` names, so a list that appends is
visited layer by layer and nothing is copied. A tool's strings borrow from the
room; a type that wants owned values owns them through its own allocator,
never the reader's. A tool's type refuses unknown fields; a refusal from the
type names the key and the layer and file that hold it, so one schema holds
across every layer and an unknown key is refused in whichever layer wrote it.
`Manifest::stack` hands the read layers to `renki_config::resolve` for a
tool's typed settings, which reads them through the same documents.

## The TOML store

`Toml` is `renki_config::Store` over `tomlfuse-parse`, reading TOML 1.1.0.
A document is the parser's index over the room's nodes, each node holding its
key's span and its value's span, and the store keeps nothing of its own: a
key, a shape and the keys a document holds are answered by the index's read
view, every value an array of tables holds as `name.0.field`. A string with an
escape is decoded into the room's decode shelf when the document is read, by
the parser's own decoding, and lent from there; one without is lent from the
text. A list is the store's handle on its node, which `items` walks, so an
array of tables is a list like any other.

Its writer edits the text at the key's value span into a `core::fmt::Write`
sink rather than reserialising, so a person's comments survive every
`config set`. A key inside an inline table is edited inside its braces, a
multi-line value is replaced whole, a line inside a multi-line string is never
taken for a key, and a key under a `[[name]]` header is edited in that
element. One parser serves the store, the stack and the deserialising, so a
manifest is never parsed two ways.

## The dump

`Manifest::dump` writes the view as the one text both readers answer in: one
line per leaf, `<dotted path> = <value>`, an array of tables indexed as
`name[0]`, the value in one canonical TOML form (a basic string with `\"`,
`\\`, `\n` and `\t` escaped, an integer, `true` or `false`, a float in the
shortest form that reads back to the same number with `.0` where that form
has no point or exponent and `inf`, `-inf` or `nan` as TOML spells them, a
date, a time or a date-time in its RFC 3339 text with `T` between date and
time and the fraction of a second as written, a one-line array),
the lines in byte order of the path. The order is found by walking the
leaves smallest first through the room's index, and the lines go into a
`core::fmt::Write` sink. `get <key>` is the dump's line for that key.

## The POSIX reader

Under `posix/` at the repository's root, beside the crates, and reading
nothing outside it: `posix/manifest.sh`, a library of POSIX `sh` to source,
and the `awk` it runs. It answers `dump <file>` and `get <file> <key>` in the
dump's bytes, and applies the same layers, include rule, order, merge, meta
and predicates. A `shell` predicate runs its command on every `dump` and
`get` that reads a use of it, as the Rust reader does; nothing is kept.

It reads a stated subset of TOML and refuses, with file and line, anything
outside it, so it reads a manifest right or says it cannot. In: comments,
trailing ones included; `[table]`, `[dotted.table]` and `[[array.of.tables]]`
headers; bare and dotted keys; basic strings with `\"`, `\\`, `\n` and `\t`;
literal strings; integers; booleans; arrays of those, on one line or several.
Out: multi-line strings, inline tables, nested arrays, quoted keys, floats,
dates and times, and `\u` escapes. What is not TOML inside the subset's own
forms is refused the same: a control byte in a string or a comment, and bytes
that are no UTF-8.

A consumer that cannot link Rust adds the repository as a git submodule at a
pinned revision, checks out `posix/` alone, and sources `posix/manifest.sh`;
the same revision is what its Rust side pins.

## The suite

`posix/conformance/` holds one directory per fixture: the manifest's files
with their layers, a `dump` file with the expected dump, or a `refused` file
with the expected refusal's line, which is the text both readers give. A
fixture outside the POSIX subset holds
both: the dump the Rust reader answers and the refusal the POSIX reader gives.
A file of a fixture that is not TOML is kept as `<name>.toml.in` and copied to
its name when the fixture is run, so the repository's TOML lint, which reads
every `*.toml`, never meets it. One test of this crate runs every fixture
through `Manifest::dump` and through `posix/manifest.sh`, and compares each
with what the fixture holds. Each reader is checked against the fixture, and
both against the same bytes, so a behaviour lands in both readers or the
fixture that pins it is red for the one that lacks it. The fixtures sit under
`posix/` so a consumer's sparse checkout carries them too.

Every refusal either reader gives has a fixture: each layer and include rule,
each form outside the subset and each invalid one inside it. Paths that hold a
space or a quote, an include through a link, and a dot file beside an include's
glob have fixtures too, and so does each of the three ways the untracked check
is skipped. A broken copy of the POSIX reader, run over the suite, turns it
red, which shows the suite can fail.

Both readers evaluate the same predicate kinds, `env`, `shell`, `not`, `all`
and `any`, and the suite holds a fixture for each: one where it holds and one
where it does not, `env` with each truthy and falsy word and a refused one.
The `shell` fixtures run through the Rust reader where `processes` is on; the
POSIX reader runs them always, through the same `sh -c`.

TOML itself is held by `tomlfuse-parse`'s own run of the toml-test suite;
this crate's suite holds the layers and the POSIX reader's subset.
